{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning, Mrs. ___ . I'm Dr. ___ , one of your neurology team members. You're here today for your second dose of methotrexate for your primary CNS lymphoma. How have you been feeling since your last discharge?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been doing okay overall. I’ve been at the rehab center and working on my strength. But I’ve noticed this numbness and tingling in my right foot for about a week now. It feels like it’s fallen asleep."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Is the numbness constant? Does it go up your leg at all?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It started just in the foot, but over the last couple of days, the tingling has moved up a bit—now it’s going up the outside of my leg, maybe halfway to my thigh. It’s not severe, but it’s bothersome."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks for letting me know. Given your history of CNS lymphoma and MS, we want to make sure this isn’t related to nerve involvement from the cancer spreading. We’ll start with an ultrasound to rule out a blood clot, since you’re also on blood thinners for your DVT."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. I wasn’t sure if it was just my MS acting up again or something more serious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2175-05-13 10:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good point—MS can definitely cause these kinds of symptoms. But because of your lymphoma, we have to be extra cautious and rule out other causes first."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2175-05-13 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We got the results of your leg ultrasound. Good news—it shows no sign of a deep vein thrombosis. Your veins look normal with good blood flow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2175-05-13 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So it’s not a clot?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2175-05-13 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, not a clot. But since the pain is moving up your leg, we’d like to do an MRI of your spine to make sure there’s no involvement of the spinal nerves by the lymphoma—specifically, we’re looking for something called leptomeningeal spread."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2175-05-13 15:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that common with my type of lymphoma?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2175-05-13 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s not common, but it can happen. The MRI will help us be sure. We’ll schedule it for tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2175-05-14 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Today you’ll be getting your second dose of methotrexate. We’ll give it through your PICC line, and you’ll also get leucovorin, sodium bicarbonate, and dexamethasone to protect your body from side effects."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2175-05-14 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I feel different after the infusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2175-05-14 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most people tolerate it well. Some feel tired or have mild nausea, but we’ll monitor you closely. The dexamethasone also helps with brain swelling and might improve your energy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2175-05-14 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You did great with the methotrexate infusion. No complications so far. We’ll keep an eye on your labs over the next few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2175-05-14 12:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. I’m glad that’s done. Any update on the MRI?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The spine MRI is back. I have good news—there’s no sign of lymphoma spreading to the spinal fluid or nerves. No leptomeningeal enhancement."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that’s such a relief. So what’s causing the pain then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The MRI did show some mild degenerative changes in your lower back, especially at L3-4, where there’s some narrowing near a nerve root. But it’s not pressing on anything. Given your history of MS, it’s possible this is a new flare of your MS affecting sensation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it could be MS, not the cancer?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. That’s actually the more likely explanation now that we’ve ruled out serious causes. We’d like to start you on gabapentin to help with the nerve pain and see if it improves."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ve heard of that. Is it safe with my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2175-05-15 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s safe and commonly used for neuropathic pain. We’ll start low and go slow to avoid dizziness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2175-05-15 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I also want to talk about your liver tests. Your AST and ALT are elevated—higher than when you were last here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2175-05-15 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from the chemo?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2175-05-15 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Most likely, yes. Methotrexate can affect the liver, and so can Keppra, which you’re on for seizure prevention. We’ve increased your leucovorin and IV fluids with sodium bicarbonate to help clear the methotrexate faster and protect your liver."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2175-05-15 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it get better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2175-05-15 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we expect it will. We’ll keep monitoring, and you’ll take leucovorin and sodium bicarbonate by mouth for three more days after discharge. Your oncologist will check your liver enzymes in a few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2175-05-15 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I noticed my dexamethasone was changed from twice a day to once a day. Is that okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2175-05-15 16:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s part of tapering down. You’ve been on it for a while, and we want to reduce side effects like high blood sugar or mood changes. Since your brain swelling is under control, we can safely lower the dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2175-05-15 16:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. And what about my stomach medicine? I saw we switched from omeprazole to ranitidine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2175-05-15 16:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good catch. Ranitidine doesn’t interact with methotrexate like omeprazole can, so it’s safer during chemo. It still protects your stomach, especially since you’re on steroids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2175-05-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your latest labs show your methotrexate level has dropped to a safe range. That means your body is clearing it well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2175-05-16 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2175-05-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Very good. It means the leucovorin and hydration are working. Your liver numbers are still high, but we’ll keep following them as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m feeling ready to go home. Will I need to come back soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, in two weeks. That’s when you’ll get your third dose of methotrexate, and you’ll also have a port placed by interventional radiology for easier access."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will the port help with the infusions?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’ll make things much smoother and reduce the need for repeated PICC lines. But you’ll need to hold your Lovenox injections the night before and the morning of that admission to reduce bleeding risk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And I should keep taking the new gabapentin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2175-05-16 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, twice a day. If it helps, great. If not, your neurologist can adjust it. Also, keep taking your other meds like Advair, Singulair, and the calcium and vitamin D."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2175-05-16 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I saw on the discharge papers that they’re checking for hepatitis C. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2175-05-16 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s just a routine check. The result is pending, but if it’s positive, it’s important to know before continuing methotrexate, since it can affect the liver more in that case. We’ll follow up with your oncologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Everything looks good for discharge. Your neurologic exam is stable, your vitals are normal, and you’re walking independently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad. I’ve been looking forward to going home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with prescriptions for gabapentin, ranitidine, and a few days of leucovorin and sodium bicarbonate. Stop the Dilaudid and omeprazole—we don’t need them anymore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I’ll see the oncologist soon for the lab follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they’ll call you about your liver tests. And we’ll see you back here in two weeks for the port and next chemo dose. Keep in touch with your husband and let us know if anything changes—like new weakness, severe pain, or shortness of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, Doctor. I really appreciate all your help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2175-05-17 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well, and we’re here to support you. Take care, and we’ll see you soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2175-05-13 09:46:00",
    "admission_end": "2175-05-17 15:13:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old woman with a history of multiple sclerosis, ulcerative colitis, and asthma was readmitted for her second dose of methotrexate chemotherapy for primary CNS diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. She reported new-onset numbness and tingling in her right foot that had extended up the lateral leg. Evaluation included a negative lower extremity ultrasound for DVT and a spine MRI that showed no leptomeningeal spread of lymphoma but revealed degenerative changes at L3-4. Symptoms were likely related to either MS or nerve root irritation, and she was started on gabapentin. Her methotrexate was administered without complication, though transaminitis developed, presumed to be drug-related. She was managed with leucovorin and sodium bicarbonate, which were continued post-discharge. Dexamethasone was tapered, omeprazole was switched to ranitidine, and Dilaudid was discontinued. She remained stable, was discharged home, and will return in two weeks for port placement and next-cycle chemotherapy.",
    "problems": [
      "Primary central nervous system lymphoma",
      "Disturbance of skin sensation",
      "Pain in limb",
      "Nonspecific abnormal results of function study of liver",
      "Multiple sclerosis",
      "Other pulmonary embolism and infarction",
      "Acute venous embolism and thrombosis of unspecified deep vessels of lower extremity",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Personal history of other diseases of digestive system",
      "Antineoplastic chemotherapy induced anemia",
      "Anemia in neoplastic disease"
    ]
  }
}
